<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The launcher icon, for API 26+ (every device this app supports in
    practice; minSdk 24 falls back to the legacy rasters in mipmap-<dpi>).

    <monochrome> was MISSING until 2026-08-29, and that is the defect worth
    naming. Since Android 13, a user who turns on themed icons gets every
    icon re-rendered from this layer, tinted to their wallpaper. An app that
    does not supply one leaves the launcher to improvise from the colour
    layers instead — and launchers improvise differently from each other and,
    on several of them, differently between light and dark. That is the
    shape of the operator's report: the icon "zoomed in and offset" in one
    theme and correct in the other, with our own resources byte-identical
    across both (verified: every mipmap-night-* file matches its light
    counterpart exactly, and an instrumented build proved BOTH themes resolve
    to this adaptive icon, not the legacy rasters).

    Honest status: this was NOT reproduced on the Pixel 7 emulator, where the
    icon measures pixel-identical in both themes (ring 159x159, mark 62x78 at
    the same offset). The missing monochrome layer is the one real, verifiable
    gap found, and supplying it replaces launcher improvisation with a
    silhouette we control — which is the permanent fix whether or not that
    improvisation is the operator's exact trigger.

    The monochrome art is generated from the SAME source and at the SAME
    geometry as the colour foreground (mipmap-*/ic_launcher_monochrome.png,
    the foreground's own alpha channel), so a launcher switching between
    treatments cannot move or resize the mark — which is precisely the
    "offset" half of the complaint.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background"/>
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground"/>
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome"/>
</adaptive-icon>
